{
  "dom_id": "step-q347901",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the first two non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+4x+4{x}^{2}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+4x+4{x}^{2})=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,\\frac{16{x}^{3}}{3}-\u22ef$",
  "worked_solution": "First, using the laws of logarithms, we observe that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+4x+4{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061((1+2x)^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Maclaurin series for $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+2x) & =(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}(2x)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(2x)^{3}-\u22ef \\\\  & =2x-\\frac{1}{2}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{1}{3}(8{x}^{3})-\u22ef \\\\  & =2x-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}-\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply the previous expansion by $2$ to obtain the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+4x+4{x}^{2}) & =2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+2x) \\\\  & =2(2x-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{3}-\u22ef) \\\\  & =4x-4{x}^{2}+\\frac{16{x}^{3}}{3}-\u22ef\\end{aligned}\n$$",
